On Thu, 4 Mar 2004, Ricardo Staudt wrote:

[YANIs]

> - Kick small monsters to throw them away (as in the last message)
> The kick has never been really useful in combat, I suggest that
> kicking a small monster would have the same result as kicking an light
> object (throwing them away), I think this is a great way to avoid
> (cock/chick)atrices when you have boots as well as escape/avoid
> annoying monsters when needed by keeping distance.

  Agree.  The question is, how much can a player kick anyway?  Sure,
cockatrice soccer would be loads of fun, but can the player kick it
very far?  Kicking monsters would have to be consistent, weight-wise,
with kicking objects, and ISTR that even just moderately heavy objects
go "Thump!" a lot.  How heavy is the average cockatrice?
  And then there's presumably the issue of dodging: you can't expect
to successfully kick a monster every time, or even most of the time,
unless it's really small and slow (newt) or so big you can't miss
(dragon).


> - Takedown/fall down
> This might also be interesting, by kick, by attacking or throwing
> something heavy (like a corpse), the opponent may [lose] his balance
> [and] fall to the ground,

  Bah.  Although, being an Infocom fan, I wouldn't mind the addition
of a sitting/standing distinction to Nethack as a "joke" feature.

  #sit
  You are sitting on the floor.
  [right-arrow]
  Maybe you should try getting up.
  >
  You bounce down the stairs.  Whee!
  #stand
  You are standing on your feet again.

Like I said, no significant or useful purpose to the feature, just
a little flavor.  Besides, it adds a new conduct: sitting conduct!
"Come back 'ere, I'll bite yer ankles off!"


> - Caring a notepad
> I believe this may have already been suggested, but in any case

  Many times.  It might make a neat addition to Hearse if enough
people recorded their highlights in a notepad that was left when
they left bones; but even then, there's rgrn, so why bother?  For
single-player Nethacking, you can just use a text editor.
  Besides, letting the user edit arbitrary text in Nethack is
basically saying you're going to put a text editor into Nethack.
And that's going a bit too far, I think.  Despite the precedent
set by Sokoban. ;-)


> - Improved #chat with certain people for certain actions
> An altar priest may charge (a considerably high tax) you to
> uncurse/bless one of your items. Shopkeepers charge you to identify
> items limited to their specialty (if it's a armor dealership, he will
> only identify armors).

  I think the first idea would never get used, and the second would
get *ab*used.  The #chat idea is very good, but these applications
seem poor IMHO.


> - Looking at the pictures have taken with your expensive camera (after
> all, it's expensive)
> I know that there is a patch for it

  I've patched my copy of Nethack with the photo patch, although I
haven't used it outside of wizmode yet.  It looks decent, and the
concept itself is outstanding IMO.  The big selling point for me is
the appearance of intelligence in the patch: it takes the complex
game situation and converts it to a simple yet compelling text message.
  I do think the #timer addition was a bit overkill, but it's pretty
cool that he got it to work, even when the camera is in a bag, or
you're hallucinating, or...

